+## Copy detection (PAPH)
+
+[PAPH 4.2](vendor/paph/README.md) fingerprints an artwork into two wires — Tier 1, 3952 bytes:
+section summaries, 128 local codes and a 32-keypoint sketch; Tier 2, 32 + 40·n bytes: up to 512
+keypoints with 256-bit descriptors — and its comparator (42) reads two fingerprints and returns a
+verdict (`Unrelated`, `Related`, `Suspected`, `Copy`, `Identical`, or `Indeterminate` when it
+abstains) with the evidence: structural agreement, the geometric models it fitted (translation,
+scale, rotation, **reflection**), their inliers, and which channels agree. Pixel art is what it was
+built for: integer upscales are divided back out, mattes folded, palettes and inversions read
+for what they are.
+
+A comparator is not an index — it reads pairs. Wiring it into search is three steps:
+
+**1. Fingerprint at ingest.** The `paph` enrichment stage runs right after `stats`, on the
+pixels it decoded and before the AI stages' memory-hungry PNG work: it hashes the artwork in
+WebAssembly — ~5–60 ms for a Pixagram-sized image — and sends the wires to the `PaphIndex`
+Durable Object. Keyed on the content hash like every stage (`artworks.paph_hash`): an unchanged
+image is a no-op. Images over `PAPH_MAX_PIXELS` are brought inside it first — an exact blow-up
+divided back to its pixels, anything else box-filtered by the smallest integer factor that
+fits — because the hasher needs ~60 bytes per pixel and an isolate has 128 MB for everything.
+When `embed` then computes the image's embedding, a second, small pass checks that vector's
+neighbours too (Vectorize does not show a vector it was just given, so the stage could not).
+
+**2. Nominate candidates with exact-match keys.** The index stores each artwork under two key
+families derived from its wires (`Engine.indexKeys`, `KEYS_VERSION` 1):
+
+| family | key | what it survives |
+|---|---|---|
+| local codes | each Tier-1 local code, folded to 53 bits | canonical under the 8 symmetries of the square and colour inversion: mirrors, rotations, inversions, crops, integer upscales, pastes |
+| descriptor bands | ten 24-bit slices of a keypoint descriptor (64 strongest keypoints indexed; a query uses all of its own, plus their mirror images) | pastes into another work, tight crops, re-sampling the codes miss |
+
+A query scores every indexed work by Σ 1/df over the keys they share (df: how many works hold
+the key; keys held by more than `PAPH_DF_CAP` works are skipped), keeps the top
+`PAPH_CANDIDATES` of each family, and adds what the other channels nominate — not cut by the
+key budget: pHash neighbours (`phash_chunks`), SigLIP neighbours (the stored embedding; the
+fresh one in the second pass), and, for an unchanged image, up to 256 previous partners, so a
+re-run refreshes their verdicts. The comparator works through the list 64 candidates at a time,
+so memory stays flat however many there are. Measured on the paph test corpus (recall@32 among 200 same-style
+distractors): either family finds the original of every mirrored, rotated, cropped, upscaled,
+inverted and pasted copy, and at least as many resampled and recoloured copies as the
+comparator itself certifies; resampling and recolouring are where pHash and SigLIP earn their
+place.
+
+**3. Let the comparator decide.** The Durable Object loads the candidates' wires from its own
+SQLite and runs comparator 42 on each, gated by the stage-1 screen (a pair the screen rejects
+is never compared). Verdicts at or above `PAPH_MIN_VERDICT` go to D1 `paph_matches` as
+(earlier post, later post) by **chain time**: the chain fixes who published first, PAPH says
+whether the later work is a copy — together, the evidence an artist needs, without either
+being a legal finding. Each verdict records the comparator and calibration identity it was
+reached under. A check replaces the verdicts of exactly the pairs it examined — so two
+artworks indexed at the same moment cannot erase each other's findings — and marks the stage
+complete (`artworks.paph_hash`) in the same D1 transaction; a verdict is written only while
+both images are still the ones it was reached on and both posts are live. When an image
+changes, its old verdicts are withdrawn before the new fingerprint is indexed (and the old
+fingerprint too, if the run fails). A run that fails is retried when the failure looks
+transient.
+
+Why a Durable Object and not D1 for the index: the comparator needs every candidate's wires
+(~24 KB each). Through D1 they would cross the network and arrive as JavaScript number arrays
+(D1 returns BLOBs that way); in the Durable Object they are read from local SQLite next to the
+engine, so a query ships one fingerprint in and a few verdicts out. It also keeps copy
+detection's CPU off the D1 database that serves search. `src/paph/store.ts` holds the logic and
+the SQL (its query plans are tested to stay on primary keys); the DO is a thin wrapper.
+
+```
+GET /copies/1                  stored verdicts — fast, D1 only
+GET /copies/1?live=1           recomputed now (admin token: after tuning, or to double-check)
+GET /copies/1/report/3?wires=1 the evidence, and the wires to reproduce it
+POST /copies-by-image          {"image":"data:image/png;base64,…"} — check an upload before minting
+```
+
+```jsonc
+// GET /copies/1 → items are search items plus `copy`
+{ "id": 1, "min": "Copy", "method": "stored", "indexed": true, "items": [
+  { "id": 3, "author": "copycat", "title": "My mask", …,
+    "copy": { "verdict": "Copy", "state": 3, "certifiable": true, "structural": 7232, "geometry": 10000,
+              "inliers": 151, "mirrored": true, "relation": "later", "same_author": false,
+              "via": ["codes", "bands"], "computed": 1791234567 } } ] }
+```
+
+**Verification.** The report endpoint returns exactly what the open-source engines return, and
+with `wires=1` the two fingerprints: re-running `paph-js` (JavaScript) or the WASM module on them
+reproduces the report byte for byte — `npm run e2e:paph` checks precisely that. A verdict the
+platform shows is one anyone can recompute.
+
+**Operations.**
+* *Backfill / first deploy*: `npm run db:migrate`, deploy, then `scripts/admin.sh reindex-all
+  '"paph"'` — each artwork is hashed once and checked against everything indexed before it,
+  so every pair is examined exactly once, in arrival order.
+* *Engine update*: when a new paph release changes `KEYS_VERSION`, `scripts/admin.sh paph-rekey`
+  until `remaining` is 0 (keys are re-derived from stored wires, nothing is re-hashed); a new
+  wire version needs `reindex-all '"paph"' true`.
+* *Alerts*: `scripts/admin.sh paph-alerts 7` — the week's cross-author `Copy`/`Identical`
+  verdicts, earlier → later, each with its report URL.
+* *Deletion*: a post deleted on chain leaves the index and the verdicts with the rest of its
+  indexes.
+
+**Costs and limits.** Per artwork: one hash (~5–60 ms CPU), ~50 KB in the Durable Object (wires
+plus ~760 key postings), ~1,500 SQLite rows written once (postings and document frequencies).
+Per check: one SQLite query over the index and a comparison per screened candidate (~2–5 ms
+each); the stage's check typically compares a few dozen candidates. One Durable Object holds
+10 GB — about 200,000 artworks; beyond that, shard `PaphIndex` by key range and fan the query
+out. All comparisons run in that one object, one request at a time, so the public routes are
+kept off it where they can be: stored `/copies` reads never touch it, `live=1` needs the admin
+token, uploads and reports are cached (per image, per image pair), and the two routes that
+compare on demand share a per-client budget (`PAPH_LIMITER`, 30 a minute). Verdicts are
+pairwise: two copies of the same original are also each other's copies, and a collage is a
+copy of every work pasted into it.
+
